    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> kingdom on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> floodplain, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a> with a princess-chief governing the south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalolo">Nalolo</a>.</li><li>Kololo invaders from the south ruled the floodplain for a generation until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> restored their kingdom in 1864.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewanika">Lewanika</a>’s treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>’s company kept Barotseland’s king and councils alive under British rule until 1964.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Silozi, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> kingdom, is mainly derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Sotho</a>, the speech of the Kololo who ruled the floodplain for only about 25 years.</p></aside>
    </section>

